the-observer\n- connections/git-repository-boundaries-should-be-explicit\n- connections/git-scope-errors-inflate-the-work-estimate\n- connections/ground-truth-checks-must-match-the-lying-layer\n- connections/hindsight-leakage-and-pregame-freeze-capture\n- connections/hook-execution-environment-path-assumptions\n- connections/inbox-triage-and-morning-briefing-taxonomy\n- connections/install-time-capture-goes-stale-when-host-moves\n- connections/install-time-environment-capture-rots-silently\n- connections/instrumentation-that-normalizes-the-thing-under-test\n- connections/keep-data-retrievable-but-out-of-standing-context\n- connections/keep-it-retrievable-not-resident\n- connections/mandatory-routing-language-outranks-runtime-capability\n- connections/memory-compiler-tier-independence-under-failure\n- connections/mrsi-noise-vs-narrative-reliance\n- connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots\n- connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact\n- connections/name-the-clearing-condition-for-every-residual-warning\n- connections/narrowest-sufficient-escalation-over-blanket-bypass\n- connections/newer-is-not-more-complete\n- connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub\n- connections/one-artifact-two-storage-regimes-pick-one\n- connections/preconditions-that-check-the-wrong-thing\n- connections/preserve-the-prior-version-before-restyling\n- connections/refuse-at-the-earliest-unmet-precondition\n- connections/refuse-upstream-or-verify-downstream\n- connections/report-the-gap-rather-than-the-fraction\n- connections/retrieval-score-vs-advisor-grounding\n- connections/reviewer-unavailability-as-a-forcing-function\n- connections/reviewers-cannot-audit-decision-order\n- connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success\n- connections/sports-tech-vendor-integration-friction\n- connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question\n- connections/step-down-to-ground-truth-when-the-surface-lies\n- connections/substitute-a-primitive-when-the-layer-is-unreliable\n- connections/test-the-layer-before-blaming-the-artifact\n- connections/the-default-is-the-real-decision\n- connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set\n- connections/the-monitor-is-the-defect\n- connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified\n- connections/the-reference-window-decides-the-verdict\n- connections/the-second-reviewer-is-a-frame-not-an-extra-pass\n- connections/unactioned-urgent-items-age-across-digests\n- connections/vald-forcedecks-cmj-data-across-projects\n- connections/vault-constraints-shape-tooling-design\n- connections/vendor-action-bypasses-os-setting\n- connections/verify-across-every-partition-the-write-touched\n- connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output\n- connections/web-scraping-skill-and-readme-doc-trick\n- connections/windows-bash-tool-no-output-powershell-fallback\n- connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine\n- connections/windows-process-env-fixed-at-launch\n- connections/write-the-decision-where-each-consumer-will-look\n\n## Candidate 1\n(they share 7 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.094; written 45 days apart)\n\n### A: connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Background Task Exit-Code Failures Can Be False Positives\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/speedprofiling-velocity-project\"\n  - \"concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-07-18.md\"\ncreated: 2026-07-18\nupdated: 2026-07-18\n---\n\n# Connection: Background Task Exit-Code Failures Can Be False Positives\n\n## The Connection\n\nOn 2026-07-18, the SpeedProfiling ticket-04 full-game data pull ran as a background R task that reported \"failed / exit code 1,\" yet the task's own log showed a clean end-to-end run — the 10 Hz cache hit, 188 play-by-play plays saved, and the expected outputs written to disk (see [[concepts/speedprofiling-velocity-project]]). This is a specific instance of a recurring class of tooling failure documented across the environment: a status/exit signal reporting failure that does not reflect the actual outcome, where the ground truth lives in a lower-level artifact (a log file, an on-disk output) rather than the reported status code.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nExit codes and background-task status notifications are intermediate signals, not ground truth. A non-zero exit code from a detached or backgrounded process can be an artifact of the process-management layer (shell wrapper, harness notification, cleanup step) rather than a genuine failure of the work the process performed. The correct diagnostic response is to inspect the actual output — the log's final lines, the files on disk, the row counts — before treating the reported failure as real. On 2026-07-18 the SpeedProfiling pull's on-disk verification (8,932,931 rows for all 61/61 athletes, 69/69 tests passing, both caches confirmed hit on re-run) established the run had actually succeeded despite the exit-1 notification.\n\nThis mirrors the memsearch silent `exit 1` failure class (2026-06-13), where a memsearch invocation exited 1 with no surviving diagnostic output — the inverse problem, where a real failure left no trace and could not be diagnosed from the status alone. Together the two cases bracket the same lesson: the exit code and the actual outcome are independent signals that must be reconciled by examining the primitive artifacts. This is the process-execution analog of the broader \"verify directly, don't trust the intermediate layer\" discipline: just as agent citations, preview-server renders, and retrieval scores can misrepresent ground truth, so can a process's reported exit status.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-07-18 (14:09): \"background task reported exit 1 but the log showed a clean end-to-end run (cache hit on 10 Hz data, 188 pbp plays saved)\"; separately logged as a lesson — \"Background task notification showed 'failed / exit code 1' even though the R script completed cleanly — check the log before assuming failure.\"\n- On-disk verification confirmed success: 8,932,931 rows at true 10 Hz for all 61/61 athletes, 188 pbp plays, 69/69 testthat tests pass, both caches hit on re-run.\n- 2026-06-13: memsearch produced a silent `exit 1` \"with no surviving diagnostic output\" — the complementary case where the exit code was the only signal and no artifact survived to reconcile it against (see [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]]).\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/speedprofiling-velocity-project]] — The project where the ticket-04 background pull produced the false exit-1 notification.\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — Documents the complementary memsearch silent exit-1 failure class.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The broader discipline of verifying against ground-truth artifacts rather than trusting an intermediate layer; the exit-code false failure is the process-execution instance of that pattern.\n\n```\n\n### B: connections/the-monitor-is-the-defect\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: When the Monitor Is the Defect\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11\"\n  - \"concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability\"\n  - \"concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-01\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Connection: When the Monitor Is the Defect\n\n## The Connection\n\nA health check, a dry-run preview, and a budget counter all exist to describe a system's state — and each has been observed reporting confidently about something other than what it was asked. The 2026-08-11 stack doctor sweep surfaced two in one session: `daily-backup.ps1 -DryRun` printed \"committed / pushed\" for git operations it never ran, because the `Log` calls sat outside the `if (-not $DryRun)` guards; and `skill-tax` reported a budget overrun by counting all 133 on-disk `SKILL.md` files with no knowledge of `skillOverrides`, making a completed cleanup look like it had never landed.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nThe remediation sequence differs from ordinary debugging in one crucial respect: **before fixing anything, establish which layer is wrong.** In the `skill-tax` case the two candidate fixes pointed in opposite directions — trim more skills, or patch the counter — so applying either first had a 50% chance of making the real state worse while appearing to resolve the alarm. The discriminating check (\"do overridden skills leak into the listing?\") had to run before any fix, and it returned 0 of 72, exonerating the skills and convicting the monitor.\n\nThis inverts the usual triage instinct. An alarm is normally treated as evidence about the monitored system; here the alarm is evidence about *either* the system or the alarm, and the two hypotheses are not distinguishable from the alarm's output alone. The dry-run bug is the strongest case: its output was not merely inaccurate but was inaccurate in the specific direction that makes it useless — a preview that reports actions it did not take cannot be used to decide whether to take them, and it briefly fooled the sweep that was auditing it.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **Dry run reports phantom commits (2026-08-11):** logging outside the `-DryRun` guard, so the preview claimed git operations it skipped. Fixed to `[dry-run] would commit`.\n- **`skill-tax` counts the wrong population (2026-08-11):** 133 disk files counted against a listing that actually contains 61; verified empirically to 60-seen/61-expected before the script was patched.\n- **Comment-count bumps count own replies (2026-08-06):** a PR monitor treated its own replies as new findings, inflating the signal it was reporting on.\n- **`launchctl list` column 2 reports only the last *scheduled* run (2026-08-31):** an accurate field answering a narrower predicate than \"is this job healthy?\", so a stale red exit code self-clears.\n- **Repo diverged nightly by its own backup (2026-08-11):** the automation committed six self-generated files each run, so a genuine `repo-diverged` alarm fired every night for a cause the alarm could not name.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]]\n- [[concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability]]\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]]\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The narrower sibling: a field that is accurate about a smaller predicate. This article covers the broader case where the monitor is simply wrong.\n- [[connections/preconditions-that-check-the-wrong-thing]] — Guards decoupled from their own result; the dry-run bug is the same decoupling applied to logging rather than to a precondition.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The general rule this specializes: the intermediate layer must be verified, including when that layer is the verifier.\n\n```\n## Candidate 2\n(they share 4 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.027; written 102 days apart)\n\n### A: concepts/automation-stack-doctor\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Automation Stack Doctor and Self-Maintaining Scheduled-Task Cycle\"\naliases: [stack doctor, KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck, post-restart health check, self-deleting scheduled task, nightly self-maintaining cycle, daily-backup.ps1, backup execution limit, cloud-check UTC bug, stack doctor ALL CLEAR]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, windows, task-scheduler, memory, infrastructure, maintenance, scheduling, powershell]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-07-27.md\"\ncreated: 2026-07-27\nupdated: 2026-08-02\n---\n\n# Automation Stack Doctor and Self-Maintaining Scheduled-Task Cycle\n\nThe automation stack doctor is a health-check routine that verifies the full local Claude Code automation and memory stack — scheduled tasks, Python venvs, memsearch, Docker/Milvus, the error log, backups, and the Google Drive API heartbeat — and reports a consolidated status. On 2026-07-27, following a Windows reboot, a post-restart stack doctor run returned ALL CLEAR, and the session restructured the stack into a self-maintaining nightly cycle: heavy reindexing was moved into the 6:15 AM backup task while the session-end hook stays a light 45-second incremental pass.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Stack doctor scope:** a single run validates all 4 scheduled tasks, Python venvs, memsearch, Docker/Milvus survival across restart, error-log cleanliness, backup freshness, and a Google Drive API heartbeat. The 2026-07-27 post-restart run returned ALL CLEAR (backups fresh at 3h, Milvus survived reboot).\n- **Self-deleting post-restart check:** a one-shot scheduled task (`KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck`, triggered at logon + 5 min) auto-runs stack doctor after a reboot, escalates any failures to `.errors.log`, and deletes itself. This is the workaround for the fact that a session cannot literally wait through a machine restart — failures surface at the next session start via the error log.\n- **Nightly reindex folded into the 6:15 AM backup task** (`daily-backup.ps1`): uncapped, uses the full `memsearch.exe` path (per the Task Scheduler PATH lesson), escalates per-path failures, and resets the hook timeout-streak counter on success. Rationale: a first-time SecondBrain book-library index is an hours-scale job that a 45-second session-end hook can never absorb.\n- **Backup task execution limit raised 30 min → 2 h** so a heavy reindex night is not killed mid-pass. The session-end hook stays light (45 s incremental) and its alarm threshold was raised 3 → 6 so it only alarms if the nightly safety net itself falls behind — a real signal, not noise.\n- **Cloud-check timezone bug fixed:** a \"-4h old\" false backup-freshness reading came from timestamps not normalized to UTC after `ConvertFrom-Json`; the check now normalizes to UTC regardless of the return type.\n\n## Details\n\nThe session's central move is an architectural relocation of expensive work off the interactive/hook path and onto a scheduled job. The recurring memsearch indexing alarm had been misdiagnosed the prior day as an \"occasional\" hiccup; on 2026-07-27 it was recognized as structural — a first-time embedding job measured in hours (one indexer had burned 81 CPU-minutes and was still running). Because a 45-second session-end hook can never complete an hours-scale index, the correct home for that work is the nightly 6:15 AM `daily-backup.ps1` task, which runs uncapped and escalates per-path failures. The session-end hook is left as a light incremental pass, and its alarm threshold is raised from 3 to 6 consecutive misses so it only fires when the nightly safety net itself falls behind. With the library index converging over a night or two while search stays live, the alarm becomes a meaningful signal rather than chronic noise.\n\nTwo supporting reliability fixes accompanied the restructure. The backup task's execution time limit was raised from 30 minutes to 2 hours so a heavy reindex night is not terminated mid-pass — a scheduled job that runs an unbounded index needs an execution ceiling wide enough to absorb the worst case. The cloud-check UTC normalization fix addressed a false \"-4h old\" reading: after `ConvertFrom-Json`, timestamps were being compared without normalizing to UTC, so a local-vs-UTC offset was misread as staleness; normalizing regardless of the parsed return type eliminated the false alarm. The `KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck` self-deleting task closes the post-reboot verification gap — because no session can survive a restart, a one-shot logon-triggered task that runs stack doctor and escalates to `.errors.log` is the mechanism by which post-restart health reaches the user at the next session start. All changes were committed and pushed to the dotclaude and skills repos with no pending user action; the first fully self-maintaining cycle (backup → snapshot → reindex → alarm reset) was scheduled to run the following morning at 6:15 AM.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The memsearch tool whose hours-scale first-index drove this restructure; the nightly reindex-in-backup-task architecture and the raised 3→6 alarm threshold are documented there as well.\n- [[concepts/windows-task-scheduler-path-config]] — The full-`memsearch.exe`-path requirement applied to the nightly reindex task is a direct application of this Task Scheduler minimal-PATH lesson.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — The architectural rationale for moving hours-scale reindexing out of the session entirely: long-running background commands do not survive a session, so scheduled jobs are their correct home.\n- [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]] — An earlier single-pass health-check-and-remediation session on the same stack; the stack doctor generalizes that periodic triage discipline into an automated, scheduled routine.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-07-27.md]] — Post-restart stack doctor session (16:40): ALL CLEAR result, `KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck` self-deleting logon task registered, nightly reindex moved into 6:15 AM `daily-backup.ps1`, session-end hook left at 45 s with alarm threshold raised 3→6, backup execution limit raised 30 min→2 h, cloud-check UTC normalization bug fixed, PR #16 merged (`59b6f2c`).\n\n```\n\n### B: connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import\"\n  - \"concepts/the-monitor-newsletter\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-22\nupdated: 2026-04-22\n---\n\n# Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\n\n## The Connection\n\nBoth substantive sessions on 2026-04-22 ran into a version of the same problem: a tool or agent produced output that *looked* fine - a citation with a plausible author list, a photo wired into HTML with a correct relative path - but was actually wrong or unverifiable through the usual checking method. In [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]], a background repair agent's citation fixes for IDs 286, 287, and 358 contained hallucinated author lists despite correctly identifying the papers' topics. In [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]], Blake Wright's photo failed to appear in the localhost preview server even though the HTML and file path were both correct.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nIn both cases, the fix was not to redo the original work but to change the *verification method* to something closer to the ground truth. The hallucinated citations were caught by extracting the actual PDF text with PyPDF2 and reading it directly, rather than trusting the agent's summarized output. The \"broken\" photo was confirmed to be fine by opening the HTML file directly in Chrome via `file://`, rather than trusting the brainstorm preview server's rendering. The shared lesson: when an agent, tool, or service produces output that looks plausible but can't be easily double-checked through the same channel, step down to a more primitive, lower-level check (raw file text, direct file rendering) before accepting the result as correct.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-04-22 (10:57): \"Background agent produced `docs/batch_r7_fixes.json` but hallucinated wrong author lists for IDs 286, 287, and 358... Used PyPDF2 text extraction to verify actual authors and corrected all three citations before committing.\"\n- 2026-04-22 (17:21): \"Photo failed to render in localhost preview — identified as a server limitation (brainstorm preview server doesn't serve image files), not an HTML error... always verify photo rendering by opening the HTML directly in Chrome via file:// URL.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]]\n- [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]]\n\n```\n## Candidate 3\n(they share 4 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.041; written 162 days apart)\n\n### A: connections/classify-the-conflict-by-the-artifacts-kind\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Classify the Conflict by the Artifact's Kind, Not by Which Machine Is Newer\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge\"\n  - \"concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge\"\n  - \"concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage\"\n  - \"concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-29.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-29\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# Connection: Classify the Conflict by the Artifact's Kind, Not by Which Machine Is Newer\n\n## The Connection\n\nFour cross-machine conflict investigations in this workspace have now produced four different correct resolutions, and in every case the resolution was determined by what the conflicting file *is* rather than by which machine wrote it last. A transform artifact gets discarded; an append-only log gets unioned; an accumulating record gets merged by key; a set keyed by identity gets deduplicated. The 2026-09-29 `RESEARCH.md` duplicate supplies the fourth kind and completes the taxonomy.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nEvery sync tool presents a conflict in the same vocabulary — two versions, pick one — and that vocabulary is wrong for most of the files it is applied to. Byte-level divergence is the only thing the tool can measure; what to do about it is a semantic question the tool cannot see. The consequence is that *no* blanket resolution rule is safe, including the two most tempting ones: \"newest wins\" destroys rows in an accumulating log, and \"keep both\" duplicates entries in an index. Classification must therefore come before resolution, and the classification is cheap — it is a property of the file's role that the operator already knows.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **Transform artifact → discard, and fix the generator.** Roughly 200 `.from-*` files were traced to a Windows save command that re-encoded text and prepended a BOM; none represented a content difference at all, and `portfolio.json` un-mangled matched on all 9 supposedly-missing updates ([[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]]). Twenty-six of thirty-six earlier conflicts were likewise CRLF-only ([[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]).\n- **Append-only log → union, keep every line.** `global-memory/transcripts/*.md` conflicts resolved identically by hand on 9/16, by a configured merge driver on 9/22, and by stripping only the conflict markers on 9/24 — three independent routes to the same output ([[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]]).\n- **Accumulating record → merge by key; newer may be smaller.** `weekly_forecast_log.csv` was *smaller* on the Mac, and a \"Mac wins\" rule would have discarded 324 Windows-only forecast rows dating to 2026-08-26 ([[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]). The same error one layer down appeared as a DuckDB loader replacing where it should have merged, deleting 12,750 Mac-only rows ([[concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key]]).\n- **Set keyed by identity → deduplicate.** Both machines added a `Flint` entry to `~/DEV/RESEARCH.md`; the resolution kept one line and dropped the other, and a union strategy would have listed the project twice ([[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]]).\n- **Opaque revisable document → hand comparison.** Three `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework` Word/Excalidraw deliverables, with the DRAFT at 1.0 MB on Windows against 0.4 MB on the Mac, were deliberately left for a human rather than resolved by any rule.\n\nTwo corollaries fall out of the evidence. First, the conflict *count* is not the work estimate: 36 collapsed to 4, and ~200 collapsed to 0 real differences — the same frame error as the git-scope miscounts in [[connections/git-scope-errors-inflate-the-work-estimate]]. Second, a merge strategy is correct only for the artifact class it was derived from, so the union driver that was the right fix for transcript logs would be a defect if applied to the router index — a reminder that [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] presumes the generator's scope has been bounded to the class it serves.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]]\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]]\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]]\n- [[concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key]]\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]]\n- [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]]\n- [[connections/git-scope-errors-inflate-the-work-estimate]]\n- [[connections/ground-truth-checks-must-match-the-lying-layer]]\n\n```\n\n### B: connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import\"\n  - \"concepts/the-monitor-newsletter\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-22\nupdated: 2026-04-22\n---\n\n# Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\n\n## The Connection\n\nBoth substantive sessions on 2026-04-22 ran into a version of the same problem: a tool or agent produced output that *looked* fine - a citation with a plausible author list, a photo wired into HTML with a correct relative path - but was actually wrong or unverifiable through the usual checking method. In [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]], a background repair agent's citation fixes for IDs 286, 287, and 358 contained hallucinated author lists despite correctly identifying the papers' topics. In [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]], Blake Wright's photo failed to appear in the localhost preview server even though the HTML and file path were both correct.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nIn both cases, the fix was not to redo the original work but to change the *verification method* to something closer to the ground truth. The hallucinated citations were caught by extracting the actual PDF text with PyPDF2 and reading it directly, rather than trusting the agent's summarized output. The \"broken\" photo was confirmed to be fine by opening the HTML file directly in Chrome via `file://`, rather than trusting the brainstorm preview server's rendering. The shared lesson: when an agent, tool, or service produces output that looks plausible but can't be easily double-checked through the same channel, step down to a more primitive, lower-level check (raw file text, direct file rendering) before accepting the result as correct.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-04-22 (10:57): \"Background agent produced `docs/batch_r7_fixes.json` but hallucinated wrong author lists for IDs 286, 287, and 358... Used PyPDF2 text extraction to verify actual authors and corrected all three citations before committing.\"\n- 2026-04-22 (17:21): \"Photo failed to render in localhost preview — identified as a server limitation (brainstorm preview server doesn't serve image files), not an HTML error... always verify photo rendering by opening the HTML directly in Chrome via file:// URL.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]]\n- [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]]\n\n```\n## Candidate 4\n(they share 8 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.137; written 8 days apart)\n\n### A: concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\"\naliases: [49 unmanifested DEV folders, backup-manifest.json 78 to 125, job count 28 to 76, manifest coverage gap, AI_Analyst token-dashboard removed, DexaProject Google Drive warning, migration left folders unmanifested]\ntags: [backup, automation, macos, keystoneos, verification, maintenance, git]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\n\nOn 2026-09-09 a KeystoneOS daily backup run returned `status: ERROR` with two problems: the `~/.claude` repo was one commit behind GitHub (blocking its push) and 49 folders under `~/DEV` were absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`. Rather than triage the 49, the user confirmed that all of them mattered — the gap was a residue of an earlier migration — and every one was added. The manifest grew from 78 to 125 entries and the backup's job count went from 28 to 76.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The gap was invisible.** Folders absent from the manifest are never backed up, and their absence produces no error that reads as urgent — only an advisory `manifest-unmanaged` class. The 49-folder hole had existed since a prior migration.\n- **All 49 were added, not triaged:** 47 as git repos (working-tree capture to the CRASHBOX SSD) and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs` — SSD copy only, no repo semantics).\n- **Two stale entries were removed** (`AI_Analyst`, `token-dashboard`) after confirming both had already moved into `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`, leaving 125 entries. Manifest hygiene runs in both directions: adding new folders and retiring moved ones.\n- **`DexaProject`'s warning was kept deliberately** — it lives on Google Drive intentionally, so its manifest complaint is an accepted permanent condition rather than a defect to silence. A separate later finding in the same day established that the *GitHub* copy of `DexaProject` still holds a patient CSV and must be deleted.\n- **The `~/.claude` behind-by-one state was resolved with `git pull --rebase --autostash`,** preserving two uncommitted edits (`settings.json`, `hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh`). Backups never auto-merge by design.\n- **Commits were made but not pushed** — `~/.claude` at `dc54c97` and `~/.claude/global-memory` at `38e462b` — with the push deliberately left to the next scheduled backup run.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the largest instance yet of the manifest-drift failure class first catalogued in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] (three folders) and re-observed at [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] (five folders). The escalation from three to five to forty-nine is informative: the earlier gaps were ordinary drift from folders created faster than manifest entries, whereas this one had a single structural cause — a migration that moved or created many directories at once while the manifest, being hand-maintained, stayed where it was. Bulk moves are precisely the event a curated list cannot survive, which strengthens the standing proposal in [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] to derive the manifest from a directory scan rather than maintaining it by hand. The default-deny guard did its job in that the folders were reported rather than silently excluded, but reporting alone is only sufficient when the report is acted on, and forty-nine entries accumulating across weeks shows the report being observed and deferred.\n\nThe removal of `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` is the neglected half of manifest maintenance. A manifest entry pointing at a folder that has moved into `_ARCHIVE` will either fail loudly (noise) or resolve to a stale path (worse), and neither outcome is caught by any check that only asks \"is every folder covered?\" The `DexaProject` decision demonstrates the third state a manifest entry can occupy: an accepted, documented exception whose warning is expected output rather than a pending task. Recording the reason — it lives on Google Drive intentionally — is what prevents a future maintenance pass from either \"fixing\" it or being confused by it, the same discipline applied to the two large graph files kept deliberately in the 2026-09-01 classification pass. Notably, the same day's repo-visibility audit found that this deliberate local exception coexists with a genuine problem at a different layer: `erash11/DexaProject` is still a GitHub repository containing a patient CSV, awaiting deletion. A documented exception on one axis does not clear the artifact on every axis.\n\nThe `git pull --rebase --autostash` choice is worth isolating as policy. The backup job pushes several repos, and a repo that is behind its remote cannot push; the tempting resolutions are a force-push (destroys remote history) or a merge (creates a commit the operator did not author). Rebasing with autostash preserves both the remote's history and the two uncommitted working-tree edits, and the stated principle — backups never auto-merge — keeps the backup system from making content decisions on the operator's behalf. This is the same restraint shown when a two-writer SecondBrain conflict was resolved by staging both sides rather than adjudicating it, per [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]]. The decision to commit `~/.claude` and `global-memory` without pushing follows the same logic in the other direction: the push is the backup's job, so leaving it to the next scheduled run keeps one owner for that action rather than racing it.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed guard that surfaced the 49 folders; this run is the strongest argument yet for its auto-derivation follow-up.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The original three-folder instance of the same failure class.\n- [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] — The five-folder `manifest-unmanaged` instance between that one and this.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The completeness metric this session reset from 28 to 76.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The job-count completeness tell, which this session extends and complicates by moving the baseline.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The SSD leg that now receives working-tree captures for 47 additional repos.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The same day's finding that `DexaProject` is also a live public GitHub repo holding a patient CSV.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The precedent for resolving repo divergence without adjudicating content on the operator's behalf.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: a clean run over a work set that was never complete.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): initial run `status: ERROR` with `~/.claude` one commit behind and 49 unmanifested `~/DEV` folders; user confirmed all 49 mattered (\"we did a migration earlier\"); 47 added as git repos and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs`); job count 28 → 76 and manifest 78 → 125 entries; `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` removed after confirming both had moved to `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`; `DexaProject` warning kept as an intentional Google Drive exception; `~/.claude` fixed with `git pull --rebase --autostash` preserving two uncommitted edits; commits `dc54c97` and `38e462b` left unpushed for the next backup run.\n\n```\n\n### B: concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Distinguishing Stale from Live Entries Before Clearing an Error Log\"\naliases: [.errors.log archival, stale error entries, error log clearing procedure, archive then clear, escalation log hygiene, live vs stale escalation]\ntags: [automation, maintenance, memory, macos, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-31.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-31\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Distinguishing Stale from Live Entries Before Clearing an Error Log\n\nThe `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log` file is the asynchronous escalation channel through which scheduled jobs surface failures to the next interactive session. Because it is append-only and read at session start, it accumulates entries from failures that have since been fixed, and a stack-doctor pass must decide per-entry whether each one still describes a live condition. On 2026-08-31 the log's entries were confirmed stale, then archived and cleared per the skill's documented procedure.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The error log is an escalation channel, not a state record.** Scheduled tasks write to it because no session exists at run time; entries persist until manually cleared regardless of whether the underlying failure was resolved.\n- **Clearing requires a staleness determination first.** Each entry must be checked against current ground truth — the relevant job's log, the artifact it was supposed to produce — before the log is emptied, or a live failure is silently discarded.\n- **Archive, then clear.** The procedure preserves the entries elsewhere rather than deleting them outright, keeping the failure history available while restoring the log's signal value for the next session.\n- **An uncleared log degrades into noise.** Because it is surfaced at every session start, stale entries train the reader to skim past it, which is the same alarm-fatigue dynamic that motivated raising the memsearch session-end alarm threshold from 3 to 6.\n\n## Details\n\nThe escalation log exists to solve a specific structural problem: a scheduled job that fails at 06:15 or 09:00 has no session to report to, so it writes to a durable file that the next session start reads. That design makes the log write-only from the job's perspective — nothing in the pipeline knows when a failure has been remediated, so nothing removes the entry. The log therefore represents \"failures that were reported at some point,\" not \"failures that are currently true,\" and any maintenance pass over it is fundamentally a reconciliation task rather than a cleanup task.\n\nThe reconciliation is per-entry and requires an independent source. On 2026-08-31 the determination was made by checking the backup log's timeline: the entries corresponded to the 09:00 manifest failure, and the 10:31 re-run had already completed clean, so every entry described a resolved condition. Had any entry lacked such a corroborating success, clearing the log would have erased the only surviving record of an unresolved failure. Archiving before clearing hedges against a wrong staleness call — the entries remain recoverable if a later session discovers the condition was live after all — and it also preserves the failure history for pattern analysis across maintenance passes.\n\nThe cost of not doing this is not data loss but signal loss. A log that is displayed at every session start and reliably contains only old, already-fixed entries stops being read, at which point the escalation channel it implements has failed silently while appearing to work. Keeping it empty except when something is genuinely wrong is what makes its non-emptiness informative.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — Defines the stack-doctor routine and the `.errors.log` escalation mechanism that scheduled tasks write to; this article covers the maintenance of that log.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The same 2026-08-31 session; the manifest failure was the source of the entries that were confirmed stale and cleared.\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The alarm-threshold raise (3→6) that addressed the same noise-versus-signal problem from the opposite direction: making an alarm fire less often so that firing means something.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — Explains why scheduled jobs need an asynchronous escalation file at all: they run without a session to report to.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-31.md]] — Stack-doctor session (10:34): `.errors.log` entries confirmed stale rather than live, archived and cleared per the skill's documented procedure.\n\n```\n\n## Output format\n\nFor EVERY candidate, in order, emit exactly one verdict block:\n\n===VERDICT===\npair: <A path> | <B path>\nreal: yes\nreason: <one sentence>\n===END VERDICT===\n\nFor each verdict where real is yes, ALSO emit a connection article:\n\n===ARTICLE connections/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: YAML frontmatter (title, tags, sources, created, updated) then\nbody with Key Points (3-5 bullets), Details (2+ paragraphs), and Related\nConcepts linking BOTH source articles plus at least one other>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated and should name the relationship, not the\ntwo topics. After all articles, emit ONE index block containing one row per\narticle created:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[connections/<slug>]] | one-line summary | adjudicated | 2026-10-04 |\n===END INDEX===\n\nEmit ONLY these blocks - no preamble, no commentary.\n"},"uuid":"1c57d26b-3945-4d96-bdad-0883b4f2a298","timestamp":"2026-10-04T07:57:52.286Z","permissionMode":"default","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"94fe4a00-ddaa-4ce1-86a4-dacf79534b2d","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
